A||N||11||Will||Shocks to double as coil overs||||1||
Z||000000||Will||09-30-2001||05:06 PM||||What shocks can I use to replace the stock units which can also eventually be used in coil-overs? Adjustability is a must. What are common modes of adjustability? What does "five way adjustable" (tokico) mean, for instance? What's adjustable besides compression and rebound? Compression and rebound in different speed ranges?||||reg||1
Z||000001||Phil||09-30-2001||06:43 PM||||I'll assume you're speaking about the rear struts- just about any of the name brand (Koni is the only exception I know of)struts can be converted to coil over later on.||||reg||1
Z||000002||artherd||10-01-2001||06:43 AM||||Konis being the EXCEPTION?!  Please explain!!!<P><BR>Ajustability wise, typically the '5-way' struts will have only five total combinations available... like 'hardness, 1-5' with both compression and expansion damping being controled at the same time.<P>Higher-end struts and shocks<P>I need to learn more hard info about this as well, especially I am looking for discretely ajustable hihg-end parts that can be adapted to the fiero (ie, a strut sized approx correctly for the 88s, then I put a fiero coilover top perch on it from Held, etc.)<P>I wonder how common the 88 fiero strut height and knuckle departure angle are?<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<BR><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000003||Phil||10-01-2001||07:04 AM||||Koni rear struts have a bulge at the top end of the cylindrical section so the threaded spring perch sleeve won't slide on. Held Motor Sports does make a rear strut that uses the Koni cartridge but is is $$$$$$. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Wannabe||10-01-2001||09:25 AM||||Konis typically only adjust damping in rebound, while some other brands adjust in jounce(compression) and rebound (extension) I don't know why Koni do it that way. As far as rear struts, ther is not much more available unless you go with a fabricated set up using an off the shelf cartridge. I've been looing into adapting the KYB AGX strut for the '82-92 F-body. The bigest problem is that the f-body strut would lose some travel as compared to the Fiero strut, and it can only be used as a coil over since there is no spring perch. On the plus side, the AGX is an excellent product that adjusts in jounce and rebound and is reasonably priced. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||VISCERAL||10-01-2001||03:20 PM|||| <A HREF="http://www.groundcontrol.com" TARGET=_blank>www.groundcontrol.com</A> <P>rear coilover kit. Don't let them sell you the kit with a 8" spring, get the 10" lenght, 2.5" diameter Eibach spring. Choose your own spring rate. I'd say maybe 300 lbs. for a v6.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Monkeyman||10-01-2001||06:53 PM||||Why not the 8" spring?  I was under the impression that part of the purpose of coil overs was to be able to adjust ride height.  I'm planning on getting a set of Ground Control coil overs this winter for 4Play.  I want to be able to keep it between stock height and approximately a 2 1/2" drop.  I'll be buying some KYB struts and knocking the lower spring perch off, then welding the sleeve in place of that.<P>Clue me in before I make a mistake.||||reg||1
Z||000007||rockcrawl||10-01-2001||08:02 PM||||Monkeyman,<BR> 8" springs will probably be way too short. I've used 12" springs on two cars and they allowed plenty of lowering. You've got to remember that a standard adjuster sleeve is 7" long, that's about 6.5" of height adjustment! Also, you cannot weld the sleeve to the strut, they are aluminum. They just slide on and sit against the mounting bracket at the bottom of the strut. ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Monkeyman||10-02-2001||03:09 AM||||According to Ground Control, they have two different setups.  One sleeve sits against the lower spring perch and the other needs to be welded on.  Seemed like it would make more sense to buy the weld on kit as that would mean that the lower perch wouldn't be in the way of wider wheels/tires.  Maybe they confused me.  I've never really seen any good pics so it's entirely possible that I'm not seeing the picture accurately.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Will||10-04-2001||11:34 PM||||Maybe I should have clarified.<P>When I mean strut, I will say strut.<P>I was asking about shocks for the front of the car.<P>The link is <A HREF="http://www.ground-control.com" TARGET=_blank>www.ground-control.com</A> <P>An acquaintance of mine has a 1LE Camaro with Koni double adjustable shocks (from the factory!) I thought that double adjustable meant adjustable in compression and rebound separately.<p>[This message has been edited by Will (edited 10-04-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||lowCG||10-05-2001||12:05 AM||||If you want coil overs in the front shouldn't you get some tubular control arms too? I wouldn't want all the load on that part of the control arm.<BR>I think Koni race shocks are fully adjustable like that.<p>[This message has been edited by lowCG (edited 10-05-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||Will||10-06-2001||01:52 AM||||Well, I was thinking of shocks which I could use like stock shocks until such time as I could acquire tubular control arms and a front coil-over conversion.<P>If they're $300 a corner, so be it, as I will only be buying two until I can design  and build my own SLA rear suspension conversion.<P>I'm drunk-typing, by the way.<P>||||reg||1
